course: dictionary
lesson: 2247
title: Cram: packed full and last-minute study
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word cram has more than one meaning, and the words around it tell you which one. «The bus was crammed with children going to the match» means the bus was packed full of people. Look for clue words like bus, full and packed.
explain: «He stayed up all night cramming before the spelling test» means he tried to learn everything in one big rush, just before the test. Clue words like all night and before the test show this. Cramming is a school word for this kind of last-minute study.
explain: Cram can also mean to eat very fast, stuffing food into your mouth. «She crammed her sandwich in before the bell» tells you she ate it quickly, because the bell was about to ring. Always swap your meaning into the sentence to check it fits.
fact: Cram is a verb: cram, crams, crammed, cramming.
fact: «Cram» means to push too many things or people into a small space.
fact: «Cram» also means to study a lot in a short time, just before a test.
fact: «Crammed with» and «crammed full» mean packed full of something.
fact: Spaced practice means small amounts of learning over several days. It works better than cramming in one night, because the brain remembers things for longer.
exercise: «The lift was crammed with shopping bags and boxes.» What does crammed mean here? | Very quiet | Empty and clean | *Packed full | Lost
exercise: Ravi stayed up late cramming for his maths test. What is he doing? | Sleeping early to rest | *Trying to learn a lot in a short time before the test | Playing games with his friends | Drawing pictures of the test
exercise: «Spaced practice works better than cramming in one night.» Which way helps you remember for longer? | Not practising at all | *Small amounts of practice over several days | Learning everything in one night | Reading the words once and never again
test: «Mia crammed her clothes into the small suitcase.» What does crammed mean? | *Pushed too many things into a small space | Folded them neatly one by one | Washed them in the sink | Lost them on the train
test: «Jake crammed his sandwich in during break.» What does crammed mean here? | Gave it to his dog | *Ate it very fast | Threw it away | Left it on the desk
exam: Which is a better way to prepare for a test? | Skip revision and hope for the best | Cram everything into the night before | *Revise a little every day over several days | Stay up all night reading
